258
00:09:06,717 --> 00:09:09,667
Which leaves the last question.

259
00:09:09,837 --> 00:09:11,635
When all these costs rise,

260
00:09:11,635 --> 00:09:14,042
who decides what you pay,

261
00:09:14,042 --> 00:09:15,861
and who profits?

262
00:09:21,517 --> 00:09:23,959
Most American homes buy power from a

263
00:09:23,959 --> 00:09:26,187
regulated utility.

264
00:09:26,337 --> 00:09:29,307
The utility cannot set its own prices.

265
00:09:29,307 --> 00:09:31,367
A state commission does.

266
00:09:31,497 --> 00:09:33,767
Texas rules put it simply.

267
00:09:33,837 --> 00:09:35,887
The cost of service has two parts,

268
00:09:35,887 --> 00:09:37,706
allowable expenses,

269
00:09:37,706 --> 00:09:40,839
and a return on invested capital.

270
00:09:41,083 --> 00:09:44,403
And here is the key detail.

271
00:09:44,503 --> 00:09:47,337
California regulators explain that

272
00:09:47,337 --> 00:09:50,513
utilities earn no markup on fuel.

273
00:09:50,653 --> 00:09:53,243
It is passed straight through.

274
00:09:53,523 --> 00:09:54,797
But on poles,

275
00:09:54,797 --> 00:09:56,590
wires and substations,

276
00:09:56,590 --> 00:09:57,604
the rate base,

277
00:09:57,604 --> 00:09:59,763
they can earn a profit.

278
00:09:59,853 --> 00:10:01,564
Other things being equal,

279
00:10:01,564 --> 00:10:04,044
a larger rate base means higher income

280
00:10:04,044 --> 00:10:06,132
for the utility.

281
00:10:06,383 --> 00:10:08,663
How big is that profit?

282
00:10:08,973 --> 00:10:10,239
In California,

283
00:10:10,239 --> 00:10:12,815
the regulator set PG and E's allowed

284
00:10:12,815 --> 00:10:15,605
return on equity for twenty twenty-six at

285
00:10:15,605 --> 00:10:17,493
nine point nine eight percent.

286
00:10:17,813 --> 00:10:19,652
Every new mile of wire,

287
00:10:19,652 --> 00:10:21,284
every new substation,

288
00:10:21,284 --> 00:10:22,937
adds to the base that the return

289
00:10:22,937 --> 00:10:24,363
is earned on.

290
00:10:24,473 --> 00:10:26,329
And the customers pay it back,

291
00:10:26,329 --> 00:10:27,351
with interest,

292
00:10:27,351 --> 00:10:28,785
for decades.

293
00:10:29,050 --> 00:10:31,800
Let me put the whole bill together.

294
00:10:31,900 --> 00:10:33,660
Old wires to replace,

295
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:35,790
and new ones to build.

296
00:10:35,860 --> 00:10:38,400
Fuel that swings up and down.

297
00:10:38,410 --> 00:10:41,022
Data centers bidding for the same power

298
00:10:41,022 --> 00:10:41,830
plants.

299
00:10:41,830 --> 00:10:44,387
And a business model that rewards building

300
00:10:44,387 --> 00:10:45,200
more.

301
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:47,560
Each one is a reason.

302
00:10:47,590 --> 00:10:48,662
Together,

303
00:10:48,662 --> 00:10:51,736
they explain why the bill keeps climbing.

304
00:10:51,983 --> 00:10:55,146
So why are America's electricity bills

305
00:10:55,146 --> 00:10:56,233
rising?

306
00:10:56,333 --> 00:10:58,318
Not because electricity got harder to

307
00:10:58,318 --> 00:10:59,083
make.

308
00:10:59,373 --> 00:11:01,629
Because the system that delivers it is

309
00:11:01,629 --> 00:11:02,429
being rebuilt,

310
00:11:02,429 --> 00:11:04,255
the fuel became volatile,

311
00:11:04,255 --> 00:11:06,717
and a new generation of machines is

312
00:11:06,717 --> 00:11:08,563
competing with your home for the same

313
00:11:08,563 --> 00:11:09,573
power.

314
00:11:09,673 --> 00:11:11,260
The light switch looks the same as

315
00:11:11,260 --> 00:11:12,313
ever.

316
00:11:12,453 --> 00:11:15,388
The economics behind it have changed.

317
00:11:15,650 --> 00:11:18,066
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

318
00:11:18,066 --> 00:11:20,947
we follow the money behind another hidden

319
00:11:20,947 --> 00:11:23,320
link of the world economy.

320
00:11:23,440 --> 00:11:25,654
This is The Hidden Economics.
